# Copy to `Caddyfile` and replace budget.example.com with your own domain.
# Used by docker-compose.proxy.yml. See docs/reverse-proxy.md.
#
# Caddy obtains and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate on its own. Nothing to
# configure for that: it just needs the domain pointed at this host and ports
# 80 and 443 reachable from the Internet.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REPLACE budget.example.com BELOW BEFORE YOU START THE STACK.
#
# Caddy asks Let's Encrypt for a certificate covering whatever name is on that
# line. Nobody controls budget.example.com, so left as shipped the request can
# never be validated and the site never gets a certificate.
#
# Nothing about that failure is loud. Caddy starts normally, logs `obtaining
# certificate` for budget.example.com, then repeats `could not get certificate
# from issuer` and keeps retrying in the background. The container stays up.
#
# In the browser the site does not load at all. There is no certificate warning
# to click past, because there is no certificate: the TLS handshake itself
# fails. Chrome and Firefox report that the site cannot provide a secure
# connection, and `curl` reports `tlsv1 alert internal error`.
#
# Testing on a LAN, with no public domain? Put this host's name or address on
# that line and uncomment `tls internal` below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
budget.example.com {
	# `budgetpilot` is the service name in docker-compose.yml, resolved over the
	# internal Compose network. Port 3000 is the container's own port, which
	# never changes (APP_PORT only affects host publishing, unused here).
	reverse_proxy budgetpilot:3000

	# Local and LAN testing only. Uncomment to have Caddy issue the certificate
	# from its own local authority instead of asking Let's Encrypt. The stack
	# then comes up with working HTTPS on a name no public authority could
	# validate, which is what lets you try the proxy without owning a domain.
	# Set ORIGIN in .env to the same https:// address you type in the browser.
	#
	# The trade-off: every browser warns on the first visit, because nothing
	# trusts that authority. That is fine while you are testing on your own
	# network. Do not use it on an instance reachable from the Internet, where
	# the only thing between a visitor and an impostor would be a warning you
	# have taught them to click through.
	# tls internal

	# The bank consent callback carries an OAuth-style one-time `code` in its
	# query string. Dropping `code` and `state` from the access log keeps that
	# secret out of a file that tends to be world-readable, shipped to a log
	# collector, or kept far longer than the code stays valid.
	log {
		format filter {
			wrap console
			fields {
				request>uri query {
					delete code
					delete state
				}
			}
		}
	}
}

# Serving several apps from one Caddy? Add another site block; each domain gets
# its own certificate automatically.
